    "engine": "knitr",
    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Choropleth maps in R with canvasXpress\"\ndescription: \"Build an honest, interactive choropleth in R with canvasXpress — join values to a topoJSON, avoid the area-bias trap, class a skewed scale with natural breaks, and pick a sequential vs diverging palette.\"\nauthor: \"Isaac Neuhaus\"\ndate: \"2026-10-06\"\ncategories: [R, canvasXpress, maps, choropleth, visualization]\nimage: \"thumbnail.png\"\n---\n\n\n\nA choropleth shades each region by a value — emissions, incidence, turnout, income. It is the most\ncommon map in data analysis and the easiest to get *subtly wrong*. The mechanics are simple: join a\ntable of values to a set of shapes by a common key, then map value to colour. The judgement is in\nthe rest — what you encode, how you class it, and which palette you choose. This post builds one in\nR with [canvasXpress](https://canvasxpress.org) and treats those choices as first-class, not\nafterthoughts. The join and the colouring happen inside the render, so every figure here is one\nportable spec you can re-run from R, Python or the browser.\n\n## First, the trap: colour encodes a *rate*, not a *count*\n\nA choropleth fills an **area**, and area is a size channel your eye cannot help reading. Shade\ncountries by a raw count — total cases, total emissions, total sales — and big, populous regions\nlight up simply because they are big and populous. You have drawn a population map with extra steps.\n\nThe fix is almost always to map a **rate or density**: cases per 100k, emissions per capita, sales\nper store. Reserve raw counts for a *proportional-symbol* map (a sized bubble at each centroid),\nwhere the size channel carries the count honestly and area is not doing uninvited work. Decide which\nquestion you are answering before you pick the geometry — this one choice prevents most misleading\nmaps.\n\n## One value per region\n\nA choropleth needs exactly one measurement per shape. In canvasXpress the **columns** are the\nregions (they match the map by name) and the **row** is the variable you colour by — here CO₂\nemissions per country for 2018. Load a prepared table; swap it for your own (any `country → value`\ntable works).\n\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nlibrary(canvasXpress)\n\ny <- read.table(\n  \"https://www.canvasxpress.org/data/r/cX-CO2-dat.txt\",\n  header = TRUE, sep = \"\\t\", quote = \"\", row.names = 1,\n  check.names = FALSE, stringsAsFactors = FALSE\n)\n\ndim(y)              # 1 row (CO2) x N countries\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n\n```\n[1]   1 198\n```\n\n\n:::\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\ny[, 1:4]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n\n```\n    Afghanistan  Albania  Algeria Andorra\nCO2    10.45267 5.402999 164.3093 0.46421\n```\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\nThe single row is the variable (`CO2`); each column name is a country that must match a shape in the\nmap. An optional **sample annotation** table (rows = countries) rides along for richer tooltips and\nlegends — here the continent and ISO code.\n\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nx <- read.table(\n  \"https://www.canvasxpress.org/data/r/cX-CO2-smp.txt\",\n  header = TRUE, sep = \"\\t\", quote = \"\", row.names = 1,\n  check.names = FALSE, stringsAsFactors = FALSE\n)\nhead(x)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n\n```\n            Code     Continent     Country\nAfghanistan  AFG          Asia Afghanistan\nAlbania      ALB        Europe     Albania\nAlgeria      DZA        Africa     Algeria\nAndorra      AND        Europe     Andorra\nAngola       AGO        Africa      Angola\nAnguilla     AIA North America    Anguilla\n```\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\n## A first map: one continuous ramp\n\n`graphType = \"Map\"` with a `topoJSON` of world boundaries draws the shapes; `colorBy = \"CO2\"` shades\neach country on a continuous scale. The countries join to the map by name — no manual merge, no\nprojection math.\n\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\ncanvasXpress(\n  data      = y,\n  smpAnnot  = x,\n  graphType = \"Map\",\n  colorBy   = \"CO2\",\n  topoJSON  = \"https://www.canvasxpress.org/data/maps/WORLD.json\",\n  mapId     = \"co2Continuous\",\n  legendPosition = \"left\",\n  theme = \"solarized\",\n  title = \"CO2 emissions per country (2018)\",\n  width  = 700,\n  height = 500\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n```{=html}\n<div id=\"htmlwidget-538e589412529d33d1a4\" style=\"width:100%;height:464px;\" class=\"canvasXpress html-widget\"></div>\n<script type=\"application/json\" data-for=\"htmlwidget-538e589412529d33d1a4\">{\"x\":{\"data\":{\"y\":{\"vars\":[\"CO2\"],\"smps\":[\"Afghanistan\",\"Albania\",\"Algeria\",\"Andorra\",\"Angola\",\"Anguilla\",\"Antigua & Barbuda\",\"Argentina\",\"Armenia\",\"Aruba\",\"Australia\",\"Austria\",\"Azerbaijan\",\"Bahamas\",\"Bahrain\",\"Bangladesh\",\"Barbados\",\"Belarus\",\"Belgium\",\"Belize\",\"Benin\",\"Bermuda\",\"Bhutan\",\"Bolivia\",\"Bosnia and Herzegovina\",\"Botswana\",\"Brazil\",\"British Virgin Islands\",\"Brunei Darussalam\",\"Bulgaria\",\"Burkina Faso\",\"Burundi\",\"Cambodia\",\"Cameroon\",\"Canada\",\"Cape Verde\",\"Central African Republic\",\"Chad\",\"Chile\",\"China\",\"Colombia\",\"Comoros\",\"Congo\",\"Cook Islands\",\"Costa Rica\",\"Croatia\",\"Cuba\",\"Cyprus\",\"Czechia\",\"Democratic Republic of the Congo\",\"Denmark\",\"Djibouti\",\"Dominican Republic\",\"Ecuador\",\"Egypt\",\"El Salvador\",\"Equatorial Guinea\",\"Eritrea\",\"Estonia\",\"Ethiopia\",\"Fiji\",\"Finland\",\"France\",\"French Polynesia\",\"Gabon\",\"Gambia\",\"Georgia\",\"Germany\",\"Ghana\",\"Greece\",\"Greenland\",\"Grenada\",\"Guatemala\",\"Guinea\",\"Guinea-Bissau\",\"Guyana\",\"Haiti\",\"Honduras\",\"Hong Kong\",\"Hungary\",\"Iceland\",\"India\",\"Indonesia\",\"Iran\",\"Iraq\",\"Ireland\",\"Israel\",\"Italy\",\"Jamaica\",\"Japan\",\"Jordan\",\"Kazakhstan\",\"Kenya\",\"Kiribati\",\"Kuwait\",\"Kyrgyzstan\",\"Lao People's Democratic Republic\",\"Latvia\",\"Lebanon\",\"Lesotho\",\"Liberia\",\"Libya\",\"Liechtens